---
title: Alcedinidae
---
# Alcedinidae

Alcedinidae is the kingfisher family, some hundred species of compact,
large-headed birds with a long dagger bill. Many catch fish by plunging from a
perch or a hover, the eye protected by a nictitating membrane as the bird enters
the water. Others live far from water and hunt insects and lizards on the
ground, so the family name is a poor guide to its habits. Nearly all excavate a
burrow in a bank and lay glossy white eggs in an unlined chamber.
See [[belted-kingfisher]].
